$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{23} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{23}{100}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }23\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }23\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }23{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083087 1074 "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.14^(―) &= 0.1444... \\\\\n&= 0.1 + 0.04 + 0.004 + 0.0004 + … \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10} + (\\frac{4}{100} + \\frac{4}{1000} + \\frac{4}{10000} + …)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series in the parentheses is a geometric series with the first term\n\n$$\na_1 = \\dfrac{4}{100}\n$$\n\nand the common ratio\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.14^(―) &= \\frac{1}{10} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{1}r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{4}{100}(\\frac{1}{10})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{4 \\cdot 10}{100}(\\frac{1}{10})^{n} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{4}{10}(\\frac{1}{10})^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186083087 1074 "The given repeating decimal can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1\\overset{―}{4} & =0.144\\,4... \\\\  & =0.1+0.04+0.004+0.000\\,4+… \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+(\\frac{4}{100}+\\frac{4}{1000}+\\frac{4}{10\\,000}+…).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series in the parentheses is a geometric series with the first term $a_1 = \\dfrac{4}{100}$ and the common ratio $r=\\dfrac{1}{10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1\\overset{―}{4} & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4\\cdot 10}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{10}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530453 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530477 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530485 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530507 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530533 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530431 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530470 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530487 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530491 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083401 1050 420.0 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083691 1071 "$7.8\\overset{―}{7}=$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083691 1071 "What is $7.87^(―)$?" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186083691 1074 "The given repeating decimal can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.8\\overset{―}{7} & =7.877\\,7... \\\\  & =7.8+0.07+0.007+0.000\\,7+… \\\\  & =7.8+(\\frac{7}{100}+\\frac{7}{1\\,000}+\\frac{7}{10\\,000}+…).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series in the parentheses is a geometric series with first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{100}$ and common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.8\\overset{―}{7} & =7.8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =7.8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =7.8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7\\cdot 10}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n} \\\\  & =7.8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186084397 1064 "A repeating decimal can be expressed as a geometric series whose common ratio is a power of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {10}\n$$\n\nFor example, the number $0.\\overline{8}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.8^(―) &= 0.8 + 0.08 + 0.008 + … \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{10} + \\frac{8}{100} + \\frac{8}{1000} + …\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\na_1 = \\dfrac{8}{10}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {0.08}{0.8} = \\dfrac 1 {10}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.8^(―) &= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{1}r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{8}{10}(\\frac{1}{10})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{n} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{-1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{n} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{-1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{n} \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8(\\frac{1}{10})^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186084397 1064 "A repeating decimal can be expressed as a geometric series whose common ratio is a power of $\\dfrac 1 {10}.$\n\nFor example, the number $0.\\overline{8}$ can be expressed as \n![](images/03/03acd5b86ea1c8f214f08482c17a8631bd8d55754d4e5ddd974fa17a9ac9cb02.svg)\nWe have $a_1 = \\dfrac{8}{10}$ and $r = \\dfrac {0.08}{0.8} = \\dfrac 1 {10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{8} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{10}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{10})}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{10})}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}\\cdot 10 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186084722 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084723 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186084722 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084724 13194139535822 17