l between $a=1$ and $x=1.1$. Plugging $x=1.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\mid \\le \\frac{M}{3!} \\mid 1.1 - \\left.1\\right|^{3};\\left. \\mid R_{2} \\mid \\mid \\le \\frac{M}{6} \\mid 0.1\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $M$, we need to compute $\\mid f^{(3)}(x) \\mid$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,1.1]$. Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\nf^{(3)}(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}f^{(3)}(x) & &= & e^{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid f^{(3)}(x) &= e^x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis an increasing function on the interval $[1,1.1]$, its value is greatest at $x=1.1$. So\n\n$$\nM = \\mid f^{(3)}(1.1) \\mid =e^{1.1}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{e^{1.1}}{6}\\end{vmatrix}0.1\\right|^{3} \\approx 0.000\\,501\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186096562 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error $R_2$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial $P_2$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|R_2| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}|x-1|^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|f^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=1.1.$ Plugging $x=1.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|1.1-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|f^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,1.1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|f^{(3)}(x)|=e^x$ is an increasing function on the interval $[1,1.1],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.1.$ So\n\n$$\nM = |f^{(3)}(1.1)| =e^{1.1},\n$$\n\