f940d99e68b5e7ea3196c51acfe2b69.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg) is between ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) and ![](images/76/76543f4805f22715457d55feb2a3c87d0d873bdcb9c1eed7a4f68539269dcf59.svg) the graph is stretched away from the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis by a scale factor of ![](images/c7/c7432bf7a520e569e53fdd1a7303ce6b9f940d99e68b5e7ea3196c51acfe2b69.svg)" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186116506 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. How can we plot the graph of $y=f(2x)$?\n\n![](images/1a/1a11bef66d0529418dde00e5713ed5e98f53a948c8a60f83fd056fd9e2c18698.png)\n\nWe can use some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ to find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(2x)$.\n\nFor example, let's take the point $(-2,0)$. The fact that it lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means\n\n$$\nf(-2)=0\n$$\n\nThen, if\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}-1}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nf(2x) = f(2\\cdot({\\color{red}-1})) = f(-2) = 0\n$$\n\nThis means the point $({\\color{red}-2},0)$ that lies on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $({\\color{red}-1},0)$ that lies on $y=f(2x)$.\n\nSo, for each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$, there will be a point on the graph of $y=f(2x)$ with the *same* $y$-value and *one half* the $x$-value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=f({\\color{red}2}x)[/math] \\\\ (-2, 0) & (-1, 0) \\\\ (0, 2) & (0, 2) \\\\ (1, 2) & (0.5, 2) \\\\ (4,-3) & (2,-3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plot the points and draw the graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(2x){:}\n$$\n\n![](images/5d/5df5e404864825a7236bc553a401cb83d43ad00d67706845e90872aca4fa5d12.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(2x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ **horizontally scaled** by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we can say that the original graph is **compressed** by a scale factor of $2$.\n\nIn general, the transformation $y=f(kx)$ scales the graph of $y=f(x)$ horizontally by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{k}\n$$\n\n- If $k$ is greater than $1$, the graph is compressed (or squished) towards the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $k$.\n- If $k$ is between $0$ and $1$, the graph is stretched away from the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $k$." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186124475 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530432 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186124476 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530438 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186126428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126429 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186126428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126430 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186126428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126431 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186126428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126432 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186126428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126433 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186126428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529665 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186126428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529925 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186126428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529990 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186126428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530042 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186126428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530163 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186126428 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529990 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186129319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530457 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186129320 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530454 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186129320 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530465 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186129320 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530493 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186129488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186129488 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = 3t, y = 4t, t ∈ [0, 1]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186129488 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=4t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186129488 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186137944 1075 #edb/ref 17592186137945 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186137944 1075 #edb/ref 17592186137946 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186137944 1075 #edb/ref 17592186137947 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186137944 1075 #edb/ref 17592186137948 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [1