\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} - 4z + 4 &=  \\\\\n(z - 2)^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186138297 1074 "We can introduce a new variable $z=x^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+4 & = \\\\ {({x}^{2})}^{2}-4({x}^{2})+4 & = \\\\ {z}^{2}-4z+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic as the square of a difference, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-4z+4 & = \\\\ (z-2)^{2} & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138298 1071 "Factor the expression $x^3 - 4x$." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186138298 1071 "Factor the expression $x^3 - 4x.$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138298 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $x$. To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x = x(x^2 - 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2 - 4$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\nx(x^2 - 4) = x(x+2)(x-2)\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186138298 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x =\nx(x^2 - 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2 - 4$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\nx(x^2 - 4) = x(x+2)(x-2)\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138302 1071 "Factor the expression $x^3 - 6x^2 + 9x$." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186138302 1071 "Factor the expression $x^3 - 6x^2 + 9x.$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138302 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $x$. To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 6x^2 + 9x = x(x^2 - 6x + 9)\n$$\n\nThe expression $x^2 - 6x + 9$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x^{2} - 6x + 9) &=  \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 6x + 3^{2}) &=  \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3x + 3^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= x(x - 3)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186138302 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 6x^2 + 9x = x(x^2 - 6x + 9)\n$$\n\nThe expression $x^2 - 6x + 9$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}-6x+9) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}-6x+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ x(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138303 1071 "Factor the expression $x^3 + x^2 - 2x$." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186138303 1071 "Factor the expression $x^3 + x^2 - 2x.$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138303 1074 "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $x$. To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} + x^{2} - 2x &= x(x^{2} + x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial $x^2 + x - 2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x^{2} + x - 2) &=  \\\\\nx((x^{2} - x) + (2x - 2)) &=  \\\\\nx(x(x - 1) + 2(x - 1)) &=  \\\\\n&= x(x + 2)(x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186138303 1074 "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x & =x({x}^{2}+x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial $x^2 + x - 2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}+x-2) & = \\\\ x(({x}^{2}-x)+(2x-2)) & = \\\\ x(x(x-1)+2(x-1)) & = \\\\ x(x+2)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138304 1071 "Factor $t^4 - t^2 - 2$." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186138304 1071 "Factor $t^4 - t^2 - 2.$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138304 1074 "We can introduce a new variable $z = t^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{4} - t^{2} - 2 &=  \\\\\n(t^{2})^{2} - t^{2} - 2 &=  \\\\\n&= z^{2} - z - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} - z - 2 &=  \\\\\n(z + 1)(z - 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (t^{2} + 1)(t^{2} - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186138304 1074 "We can introduce a new variable $z = t^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-2 & = \\\\ {({t}^{2})}^{2}-{t}^{2}-2 & = \\\\ {z}^{2}-z-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-z-2 & = \\\\ (z+1)(z-2) & = \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138305 1071 "Factor $2p^4 + 5p^2 + 3$." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186138305 1071 "Factor $2p^4 + 5p^2 + 3.$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138305 1074 "We can introduce a new variable $t = p^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2p^{4} + 5p^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(p^{2})^{2} + 5p^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 2t^{2} + 5t + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2t^{2} + 5t + 3 &=  \\\\\n(t + 1)(2t + 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (p^{2} + 1)(2p^{2} + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186138305 1074 "We can introduce a new variable $t = p^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{p}^{4}+5{p}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ 2{({p}^{2})}^{2}+5{p}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+5t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+5t+3 & = \\\\ (t+1)(2t+3) & = \\\\ ({p}^{2}+1)(2{p}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138385 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530423 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138385 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530504 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138386 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530489 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138386 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530528 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138409 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530422 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138409 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530483 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138410 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530484 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530539 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138412 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530437 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138412 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530520 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138637 1050 540.0 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186138638 1050 600.0 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186139166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186139166 1071 "Factor the expression $3x^{3} + 2x^{2} - x$." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186139166 1071 "Factor the expression $3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x.$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186139166 1074 "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x & =x(3{x}^{2}+2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial $3{x}^{2}+2x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3{x}^{2}+2x-1) & = \\\\ x((3{x}^{2}+3x)+(-x-1)) & = \\\\ x(3x(x+1)-1(x+1)) & = \\\\ x(3x-1)(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186139168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186139168 1071 "Factor $x^{4} - x^{2} - 6$." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186139168 1071 "Factor ${x}^{4}-{x}^{2}-6.$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186139168 1074 "We can introduce a new variable $z={x}^{2}$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}-6 & ={({x}^{2})}^{2}-({x}^{2})-6 \\\\  & ={z}^{2}-z-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-z-6 & =(z+2)(z-3) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2)({x}^{2}-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186139643 1064 "Suppose that we want to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nt^4+4t^2+3.\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is a quartic, but it can also be viewed as a quadratic \"in disguise.\" To see this, notice that if we introduce a new variable $z=t^2,$ then we can rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ ({t}^{2})^{2}+4({t}^{2})+3 & = \\\\ {z}^{2}+4z+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}+4z+3 & = \\\\ (z+1)(z+3) & = \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $t^4+4t^2+3$ factors into $(t^2+1)(t^2+3).$ We can double-check this result by multiplying it out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}+3) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}({t}^{2}+3)+1({t}^{2}+3) & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+3{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}+3 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186139643 1064 "Suppose that we want to factor the following trinomial:\n$t^4+4t^2+3$.\n\nThis trinomial is a quartic, but it can also be viewed as a quadratic \"in disguise.\" To see this, notice that if we introduce a new variable $z=t^2$, then we can rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{4} + 4t^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n(t^{2})^{2} + 4(t^{2}) + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= z^{2} + 4z + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} + 4z + 3 &=  \\\\\n(z + 1)(z + 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (t^{2} + 1)(t^{2} + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $t^4+4t^2+3$ factors into $(t^2+1)(t^2+3)$. We can double-check this result by multiplying it out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(t^{2} + 1)(t^{2} + 3) &=  \\\\\nt^{2}(t^{2} + 3) + 1(t^{2} + 3) &=  \\\\\nt^{4} + 3t^{2} + t^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= t^{4} + 4t^{2} + 3 \\mid ✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186139645 1064 "To factor a polynomial, the first step is to find the common factor of all the terms and factor it out. Once we've done that, we can try to apply other factoring tricks that we've learned for quadratic expressions.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $2x^3-2x$.\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $2x$. To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $2x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n2x^3-2x = 2x(x^2-1)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n2x(x^2-1) = 2x(x+1)(x-1)\n$$\n\nSo, the expression $2x^3-2x$ factors into $2x(x+1)(x-1)$." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186139645 1064 "To factor a polynomial, the first step is to find the common factor of all the terms and factor it out. Once we've done that, we can try to apply other factoring tricks that we've learned for quadratic expressions.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $2x^3-2x.$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $2x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $2x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2x^3-2x = 2x(x^2-1)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n2x(x^2-1) = 2x(x+1)(x-1)\n$$\n\nSo, the expression $2x^3-2x$ factors into $2x(x+1)(x-1).$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186157445 1038 #edb/ref 175