}{2n-1}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{2(n+1)-1}}{2(n+1)-1}\\cdot \\frac{2n-1}{(x-1)^{2n-1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{2n+1}}{2n+1}\\cdot \\frac{2n-1}{(x-1)^{2n-1}}| \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2n-1}{2n+1}) \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{(2-\\frac{1}{n})}{(2+\\frac{1}{n})}) \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-1{|}^{2} & <1 \\\\ |x-1| & <1 \\\\ -1<x-1 & <1 \\\\ 0<x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(0,2).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x-1|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{((0)-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}-⋯ \\\\  & =-(1+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}+⋯)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series in parentheses with ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},$ we see that the series is divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- When $x=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{((2)-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series with ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},$ we see that the series is divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\nSo the interval of convergence is $I=(0,2).$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186178239 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178240 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186178239 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178241 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186178239 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178242 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186178239 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178243 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186178239 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178244 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186178239 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529621 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186178239 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529853 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186178239 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530020 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186178239 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530025 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186178239 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530087 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186178239 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530087 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186180095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186180095 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x \\ge 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $xm$, the velocity is $vm/s$, and the acceleration is $k(1 - x)m/s^{2}$, where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v = \\sqrt{6}m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v = 3m/s$ when its displacement is $\\frac{3}{2}m$, find the maximum velocity of the particle." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186180095 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k(1-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,m,$ find the maximum velocity of the particle." 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186180095 1074 "![](images/75/75ef4755be2965b674fc7a5440ff05bb3f37d70507941eb48eeae554fcb2d538.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(1-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(1-x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(1-x) & =0 \\\\ 1-x & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=1\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $\\frac{k}{2}>0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{k}{2}<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=1\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(1-x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =k(0-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+3.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ when $x=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(3)^{2} & =k(\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & =\\frac{3}{8}k+3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}k & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-2{x}^{2}+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-4{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{8x-4{x}^{2}+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{8x-4{x}^{2}+6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(1)=\\sqrt{8(1)-4(1)^{2}+6}=\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s.\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186180098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178257 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186186624 1078 #edb/ref 17592186186627 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186188043 1071 "![](images/d4/d405cc4f96436baa6a90b8b8632a105efe7875f296e800db9ae846c1ee9eb805.svg)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\textrm{Var}\\big[X \\,|\\, Y=-2\\big]$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, -2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $\\mu_{X|-2}=\\dfrac{3}{4}.$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186188043 1071 "${3x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $\\textrm{Var}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}X \\, & \\, Y=-2\\big\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, -2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $\\mu_{X \\mid -2}=\\dfrac{3}{4}$." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186188043 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(X - μ_{X \\mid y})^{2} & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}X^{2} & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{X \\mid y}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}X^2 \\, & \\, Y=y \\big\\end{bmatrix} = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x^2 \\, f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE\\begin{bmatrix}X^{2} & Y =-2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}x^{2}f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid - 2)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot 3x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}3x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{3x^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y =-2\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}X^{2} & Y =-2\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{X \\mid - 2}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5} - (\\frac{3}{4})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5} - \\frac{9}{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{80}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186188043 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[X^2 \\,| \\, Y=y \\big] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x^2 \\, f_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, y) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=-2] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=-2] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=-2]-{\\mu }_{X|-2}^