,$ compute ${∇}_{x,y}\\,f.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{x,y}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204559 1074 "To compute ${∇}_{x,y}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $y$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+y{z}^{2}) & =2xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+y{z}^{2}) & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,y}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy \\\\ {x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204563 1071 "Given the function $f(x; w) = (w_{1}x_{1} + w_{2}x_{2})^{2}$, compute $∇_{w}(∇_{w}f)$.\n\n| $∇_{w}(∇_{w}f) =$ |  | $2x_{1}^{2}$ | $2x_{1}x_{2}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186204563 1071 "Given the function $f(x;w)=({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2})^{2},$ compute ${∇}_{w}({∇}_{w}\\,f).$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}({∇}_{w}\\,f)=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$2{x}_{1}^{2}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204563 1074 "First, we compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2})^{2}=2({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}){x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2})^{2}=2({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}){x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}}=2{x}_{1}^{2}\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}}=2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}}=2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}=2{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}({∇}_{w}\\,f) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2} & 2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204567 1071 "Given the function\n$f(x; w) = w_{1}x_{1} + w_{2}x_{2} + w_{3}x_{3}$,\ncompute $∇_{w}f$ with the input $x = [1, 2, 3]^{T}$.\n\n| $∇_{w}f =$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186204567 1071 "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $x={[\\begin{aligned}1,\\,2,\\,3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204567 1074 "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{3}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{3}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $x={[\\begin{aligned}1,\\,2,\\,3\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204574 1071 "Consider the function $f$, defined as\n$f(x_{1}, x_{2}) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2}$.\nWhat is the shape of the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186204574 1071 "Consider the function $f,$ defined as \n\n$$\nf({x}_{1},{x}_{2})={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the shape of the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204574 1074 "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ and\n\n- outputs a scalar $f({x}_{1},{x}_{2})\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the total derivative will be a row vector of shape $1\\times 2.$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204575 1071 "Consider a function $f: R^{3 \\cdot 2} → R^{3}$ defined by\n$f(A) = A\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nGiven that $a_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have$\\frac{∂f_{3}}{∂a_{32}} =$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186204575 1071 "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=A[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{32}}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204575 1074 "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\nf(A)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}+2{a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21}+2{a}_{22} \\\\ {a}_{31}+2{a}_{32}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{32}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{32}}({a}_{31}+2{a}_{32})=2.\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204580 1071 "Consider the function $f: R → R^{2}$, defined by\n$f(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$?" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186204580 1071 "Consider the function $f:R\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186204580 1074 "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{2}$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205027 1061 463319 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205027 1061 655778 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205028 1061 463320 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205028 1061 655779 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205029 1061 463321 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205029 1061 655780 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205030 1061 463324 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205030 1061 655783 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205031 1061 463325 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205031 1061 655784 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205032 1061 463328 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205032 1061 655787 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205033 1061 463331 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205033 1061 655790 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205034 1061 463332 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205034 1061 655791 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205181 1064 "Recall that the gradient of a function\n$f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$\ntells us how the function changes with respect to each of its input variables. It collects all the partial derivatives into a single vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis vector points in the direction of the *steepest increase* of the function, and its components describe how sensitive $f$ is to changes in each variable $x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n$.\n\nIn other words, $\\nabla f$ gives us a complete picture of how $f$ behaves locally around a point, not just in one direction, but in every direction.\n\nBut sometimes, we’re not interested in how the function behaves in every direction. Maybe some variables are being held fixed, or we just care about how the function responds to changes in a few specific inputs. In that case, we don’t need the full gradient. We only need the derivatives with respect to the variables we care about.\n\nThis idea leads us to the concept of the **subset gradient**, which captures how a function changes with respect to only a chosen group of variables while treating the others as constant.\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn what subset gradients are, and how to compute them. Let's look at a concrete example in the next slide." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205181 1064 "Recall that the gradient of a function \n\n$$\nf : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\ntells us how the function changes with respect to each of its input variables. It collects all the partial derivatives into a single vector:\n\n![](images/fe/fe9b4aa3c2e46dceeb10f63c021232f88ff1d3bd71f40fbd464b2ddb1cabef4a.svg) \n\nThis vector points in the direction of the *steepest increase* of the function, and its components describe how sensitive $f$ is to changes in each variable $x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n.$\n\nIn other words, $\\nabla f$ gives us a complete picture of how $f$ behaves locally around a point, not just in one direction, but in every direction.\n\nBut sometimes, we’re not interested in how the function behaves in every direction. Maybe some variables are being held fixed, or we just care about how the function responds to changes in a few specific inputs. In that case, we don’t need the full gradient. We only need the derivatives with respect to the variables we care about.\n\nThis idea leads us to the concept of the **subset gradient**, which captures how a function changes with respect to only a chosen group of variables while treating the others as constant.\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn what subset gradients are, and how to compute them. Let's look at a concrete example in the next slide." 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205182 1064 "A **subset gradient** is the vector formed by taking only the partial derivatives of a function with respect to a chosen subset of variables.\n\nLet’s look at an example to understand how a subset gradient works in practice.\n\nSuppose we have the function\n\n$$\nf(x, y, z) = x^2 y + y z^2\n$$\n\nThis function depends on three variables, and its full gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe full gradient tells us how $f$ responds to changes in all three directions. But in some situations, we may only care about how $f$ changes when we vary a few of the variables, for example, just $x$ and $y$.\n\nIf that’s the case, we compute a subset gradient, which focuses only on the variables of interest. In this case, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, we are treating $z$ as a constant, as if it were fixed and not changing, while taking partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $y$ only. This is the same idea that underlies partial derivatives more generally: we hold all other variables constant except the ones we are differentiating with respect to.\n\nSubset gradients are especially useful in problems where only some variables are being updated. They allow us to focus on the directions that matter for a particular step or subproblem, without being distracted by variables we are not actively changing." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205182 1064 "A **subset gradient** is the vector formed by taking only the partial derivatives of a function with respect to a chosen subset of variables.\n\nLet’s look at an example to understand how a subset gradient works in practice.\n\nSuppose we have the function\n\n$$\nf(x, y, z) = x^2 y + y z^2.\n$$\n\nThis function depends on three variables, and its full gradient is\n\n![](images/82/823f3ea4fad73ab43a55bfc21aaacdfaf5a2c27cf69f77ce6d1fdac22af38175.svg)\n\nThe full gradient tells us how $f$ responds to changes in all three directions. But in some situations, we may only care about how $f$ changes when we vary a few of the variables, for example, just $x$ and $y.$\n\nIf that’s the case, we compute a subset gradient, which focuses only on the variables of interest. In this case, we compute\n\n![](images/33/3354ae8715103231292a40a2d042fb96ae29148c267f6960dad14e55fc2590f6.svg)\n\nHere, we are treating $z$ as a constant, as if it were fixed and not changing, while taking partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $y$ only. This is the same idea that underlies partial derivatives more generally: we hold all other variables constant except the ones we are differentiating with respect to.\n\nSubset gradients are especially useful in problems where only some variables are being updated. They allow us to focus on the directions that matter for a particular step or subproblem, without being distracted by variables we are not actively changing." 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205183 1064 "So far, we have looked at functions with a few individual variables like $x$, $y$, and $z$. But in many real-world problems, we work with vectors of inputs and parameters rather than just a few named variables.\n\nLet’s return to a familiar example where we fit a straight line to some data.\n\nSuppose our *linear model* is given by\n\n$$\nf(x; m, b) = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $x$ is the input, and\n- $m$ and $b$ are the *parameters* we are trying to learn.\n\nIf we want to understand how the output changes when we adjust $m$ and $b$, we compute the gradient with respect to these parameters, denoted $\\nabla_{m,b} \\, f$. This tells us how sensitive the function is to changes in the slope and intercept, assuming $x$ is held fixed.\n\nThe same idea applies in higher dimensions.\n\nNow, suppose we have multiple input variables and define a model\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}; \\mathbf{w}) = w_1 x_1 + w_2 x_2 + \\cdots + w_n x_n\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\mathbf{x} = (x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n)$ is the **input vector**, and\n- $\\mathbf{w} = (w_1, w_2, \\dots, w_n)$ is the **vector of parameters** (or weights).\n\nTo understand how $f$ depends on the parameters, we compute the *gradient* with respect to $\\mathbf{w}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂w_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂w_{2}} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂w_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEach $x_j$ is treated as constant during this computation. Just like in the scalar case, we are asking how the output depends on each parameter while keeping the input fixed. This gradient gives exactly the information we need when updating model weights during training.\n\nNext, we will apply this result to a specific numerical example." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205183 1064 "So far, we have looked at functions with a few individual variables like $x$, $y$, and $z$. But in many real-world problems, we work with vectors of inputs and parameters rather than just a few named variables.\n\nLet’s return to a familiar example where we fit a straight line to some data.\n\nSuppose our *linear model* is given by \n\n$$\nf(x; m, b) = mx + b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $x$ is the input, and\n\n- $m$ and $b$ are the *parameters* we are trying to learn.\n\nIf we want to understand how the output changes when we adjust $m$ and $b$, we compute the gradient with respect to these parameters, denoted $\\nabla_{m,b} \\, f$. This tells us how sensitive the function is to changes in the slope and intercept, assuming $x$ is held fixed.\n\nThe same idea applies in higher dimensions.\n\nNow, suppose we have multiple input variables and define a model  \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}; \\mathbf{w}) = w_1 x_1 + w_2 x_2 + \\cdots + w_n x_n,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\mathbf{x} = (x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n)$ is the **input vector**, and\n\n- $\\mathbf{w} = (w_1, w_2, \\dots, w_n)$ is the **vector of parameters** (or weights).\n\nTo understand how $f$ depends on the parameters, we compute the *gradient* with respect to $\\mathbf{w}{:}$\n\n![](images/63/6334e1e6f6aa114a24cfaa586fe603f4bdfda8e0bffd118c7278b858bce527f7.svg)\n\nEach $x_j$ is treated as constant during this computation. Just like in the scalar case, we are asking how the output depends on each parameter while keeping the input fixed. This gradient gives exactly the information we need when updating model weights during training.\n\nNext, we will apply this result to a specific numerical example." 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205184 1064 "Just as we can compute gradients with respect to a subset of variables, we can also take second derivatives while focusing on just the variables that matter for our problem.\n\nWhen we write $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}}(\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}} f)$, we are computing all second-order partial derivatives of the function $f$ with respect to the variables in $\\mathbf{w}$. The result is a square matrix where each entry describes how one component of the gradient changes when we vary one of the selected variables. This matrix is the **Hessian with respect to** $\\mathbf{w}$.\n\nTo make this concrete, suppose we have a function $f(x, y, z)$ that depends on three variables. If we only care about second derivatives with respect to $x$ and $y$, we write $\\nabla_{x,y}(\\nabla_{x,y} f)$.\n\nThis gives us a $2 \\times 2$ matrix that contains the following second-order partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEven though $f$ depends on three variables, this matrix isolates the second-derivative behavior of $f$ in just the $x$ and $y$ directions. This allows us to analyze or optimize over part of the input space without involving every variable.\n\nLet’s go through a concrete example.\n\nConsider the function\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}; \\mathbf{w}) = w_1^2 x_2^2 - w_2 x_1\n$$\n\nand suppose we want to compute the Hessian with respect to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w} = [w_1, \\, w_2]^T\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂w_{1}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂w_{1}}(w_{1}^{2}x_{2}^{2} - w_{2}x_{1}) = 2w_{1}x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂w_{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂w_{2}}(w_{1}^{2}x_{2}^{2} - w_{2}x_{1}) =-x_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{1}^{2}} &= 2x_{2}^{2}, & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{1}∂w_{2}}|= 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{2}∂w_{1}}|= 0 & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{2}^{2}}|= 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting everything into matrix form, we get the Hessian\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{1}∂w_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{2}∂w_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂w_{2}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is the Hessian with respect to the parameter vector $\\mathbf{w}$. It tells us how the gradient of the function (with respect to parameters) changes when we vary each parameter and reveals the local shape of the function near any given point." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205184 1064 "Just as we can compute gradients with respect to a subset of variables, we can also take second derivatives while focusing on just the variables that matter for our problem. \n\nWhen we write $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}}(\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}} f)$, we are computing all second-order partial derivatives of the function $f$ with respect to the variables in $\\mathbf{w}.$ The result is a square matrix where each entry describes how one component of the gradient changes when we vary one of the selected variables. This matrix is the **Hessian with respect to** $\\mathbf{w}.$\n\nTo make this concrete, suppose we have a function $f(x, y, z)$ that depends on three variables. If we only care about second derivatives with respect to $x$ and $y$, we write $\\nabla_{x,y}(\\nabla_{x,y} f).$\n\nThis gives us a $2 \\times 2$ matrix that contains the following second-order partial derivatives\n\n![](images/89/895686eb22d040a761ca4edfb8d5b65ca23c23cb06aadc63ce05be06a66d193c.svg)\n\nEven though $f$ depends on three variables, this matrix isolates the second-derivative behavior of $f$ in just the $x$ and $y$ directions. This allows us to analyze or optimize over part of the input space without involving every variable.\n\nLet’s go through a concrete example.  \n\nConsider the function\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}; \\mathbf{w}) = w_1^2 x_2^2 - w_2 x_1,\n$$\n\nand suppose we want to compute the Hessian with respect to $\\mathbf{w} = [w_1, \\, w_2]^T.$\n\nFirst, we compute the gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}^{2}{x}_{2}^{2}-{w}_{2}{x}_{1})=2{w}_{1}{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}^{2}{x}_{2}^{2}-{w}_{2}{x}_{1})=-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}} & =2{x}_{2}^{2}, & \\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}} & =0 & \\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting everything into matrix form, we get the Hessian\n\n![](images/52/52bd7d2b21c9d884017376168f82fe4612157f0d92802d78e98d72b90f6f1658.svg)\n\nThis is the Hessian with respect to the parameter vector $\\mathbf{w}.$ It tells us how the gradient of the function (with respect to parameters) changes when we vary each parameter and reveals the local shape of the function near any given point." 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205188 1064 "In this lesson, we’ll continue exploring the *total derivative* of a vector-valued function, which generalizes the derivative to functions with multiple inputs and outputs.\n\nSuppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x}$, then the **total derivative** of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})$, is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is sometimes called the **Jacobian matrix** of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x}$. Each row corresponds to the derivative of one component function $f_i$, and each column corresponds to the rate of change of all the components of $\\boldsymbol f$ with respect to one input variable $x_j$. This is sometimes called the **numerator layout** for derivatives because each row corresponds to each function component, which appears in the numerator positions of the partial derivatives.\n\nNotice that if $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a scalar-valued function, then the total derivative is a row vector given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, if $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is a vector-valued function of a single variable, then the total derivative is a column vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{df_{1}}{dx} \\\\ \\frac{df_{2}}{dx} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{df_{m}}{dx}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow let’s get some practice by working through a few examples of total derivatives." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205188 1064 "In this lesson, we’ll continue exploring the *total derivative* of a vector-valued function, which generalizes the derivative to functions with multiple inputs and outputs.\n\nSuppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/cd/cd9132d6a0e934349ae58a0718e6320cba3dd8ae84de7df2e3fb98b391256b57.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the **total derivative** of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/c8/c8fbc309a129b8e19db46aebff637e47259b629ef83c282a38b3b318c3a4f269.svg)\n\nThis is sometimes called the **Jacobian matrix** of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x}$. Each row corresponds to the derivative of one component function $f_i$, and each column corresponds to the rate of change of all the components of $\\boldsymbol f$ with respect to one input variable $x_j$. This is sometimes called the **numerator layout** for derivatives because each row corresponds to each function component, which appears in the numerator positions of the partial derivatives.\n\nNotice that if $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a scalar-valued function, then the total derivative is a row vector given by\n  ![](images/6e/6ef756e82eb71d528a8f279863fd86efc8669658456a65879fba73d08e964bd5.svg)\n\nOn the other hand, if $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is a vector-valued function of a single variable, then the total derivative is a column vector\n  ![](images/cd/cd232968d5ffff3040294f8093a42c91bccd3207f91bfc2f784cb53cad00441d.svg)\n\nNow let’s get some practice by working through a few examples of total derivatives." 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205189 1064 "We've learned that the derivative of a vector-valued function is a Jacobian matrix. Now imagine the input to our function $\\mathbf f$ is a matrix instead of a vector. In such cases, the Jacobian matrix must expand to account for two input dimensions.\n\nSuppose we have a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}^p$ that maps a matrix $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{A}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$, defined as\n\n![](images/f7/f7b9ca5880f164c28b490fd4a215a56769e426bdde8111a24e6530c03d83b937.svg)\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a scalar-valued function of all the entries of the matrix $\\mathbf{A}.$\n\nSince the input $\\mathbf{A}$ varies along two dimensions (rows and columns), and the output $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{A})$ varies along one (a vector with $p$ components), the total derivative of $\\mathbf f,$ which we denote as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{A}}\n$$\n\nis naturally represented as a *three-dimensional object!* The technical name for this object is a **tensor**. Since it is a three-dimensional object, this particular tensor is a **third-order tensor** (or $3$-**tensor**).\n\nTo capture a meaningful derivative, we must differentiate each component $f_i$ with respect to *every element of the matrix* $\\mathbf A.$ We can visualize this as a stack of matrices, or layers, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d533d4d2e387c8c9f177b9dafe939b58c1f1d7db2f03e9df2059fa9f7cbe22c5.png)\n\nLet's apply this concept to a specific example in the next slide." 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205189 1064 "We've learned that the derivative of a vector-valued function is a Jacobian matrix. Now imagine the input to our function $\\mathbf f$ is a matrix instead of a vector. In such cases, the Jacobian matrix must expand to account for two input dimensions.\n\nSuppose we have a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}^p$ that maps a matrix $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{A}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} & ⋯ & a_{1n} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22} & ⋯ & a_{2n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ a_{m1} & a_{m2} & ⋯ & a_{mn}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a scalar-valued function of all the entries of the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$.\n\nSince the input $\\mathbf{A}$ varies along two dimensions (rows and columns), and the output $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{A})$ varies along one (a vector with $p$ components), the total derivative of $\\mathbf f$, which we denote as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{A}}\n$$\n\nis naturally represented as a *three-dimensional object!* The technical name for this object is a **tensor**. Since it is a three-dimensional object, this particular tensor is a **third-order tensor** (or $3$-**tensor**).\n\nTo capture a meaningful derivative, we must differentiate each component $f_i$ with respect to *every element of the matrix* $\\mathbf A$. We can visualize this as a stack of matrices, or layers, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d533d4d2e387c8c9f177b9dafe939b58c1f1d7db2f03e9df2059fa9f7cbe22c5.png)\n\nLet's apply this concept to a specific example in the next slide." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205193 1064 "Let’s examine examples of functions defined by a matrix of constants multiplied by a vector more carefully.\n\nConsider the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{2} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ defined by\n![](images/f8/f81cbd9b797d1f1b05edd5f254d6735456e937355fe0e6692a4a830da925351b.svg) \n\nSuppose we want to compute the total derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}$ at the point ![](images/cd/cd0b094634acde36ee93c2fb90c6f11ab56c2f675d27ba65077542d75cb451d6.svg).\n\nThe total derivative of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^m$ with respect to a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is the $m \\times n$ matrix\n![](images/63/6305cfe857f2228d471b85f859302a43046470f1d182b34abbf3ab4b362e7e07.svg)\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ where ![](images/a8/a8d36d7195af007d90a4f62cf213e28c7f51ced97168fb16d31a2c9192799b53.svg), we get\n![](images/79/79110c30e4f827ccc401c0d8d9b8a08f35a88d12781d83c7e365171de0bbd67d.svg)\n\nFrom here, we have \n\n$$\nf_1(\\mathbf{x}) = 3x_1 + 0 \\cdot x_2, \\qquad f_2(\\mathbf{x}) = x_1 - x_2,\\qquad f_3(\\mathbf{x}) = 0 \\cdot x_1 + 2x_2.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is independent of $\\mathbf x,$ we also have\n\n![](images/c1/c187256b1ad2181f53482829d4d5076b0b09fc19f1d399aa41fb9da6ca0bc8f3.svg)\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}$ is constant and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n,$ then\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = A}.\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186205193 1064 "Let’s examine examples of functions defined by a matrix of constants multiplied by a vector more carefully.\n\nConsider the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{2} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ defined by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose we want to compute the total derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}\n$$\n\nat the point $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThe total derivative of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^m$ with respect to a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFrom here, we have\n\n$$\nf_1(\\mathbf{x}) = 3x_1 + 0 \\cdot x_2, \\qquad f_2(\\mathbf{x}) = x_1 - x_2,\\qquad f_3(\\mathbf{x}) = 0 \\cdot x_1 + 2x_2\n$$\n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{3}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{3}}{∂x_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is independent of $\\mathbf x$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}$ is constant and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$, then\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = A}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205216 1064 "Suppose we have the function $\\mathbf f: \\mathbb R^{3\\times2} \\to \\mathbb R^{2}$, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22} \\\\ a_{31} & a_{32}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the derivatives of $\\mathbf f$ layer by layer as follows:\n\n- **Layer 1**: The derivatives of the first component $f_1$ with respect to $\\mathbf A$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂a_{11}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂a_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂a_{21}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂a_{22}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂a_{31}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂a_{32}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Layer 2**: The derivatives of the second component $f_2$ with respect to $\\mathbf A$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂a_{11}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂a_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂a_{21}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂a_{22}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂a_{31}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂a_{32}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nCombining these layers into a 3D block, we get the total derivative (a $3$-tensor in $\\mathbb R^{2\\times3\\times2}$):\n\n![](images/3d/3d6eeebb92218d4f0995dbccd74b444da112f0e55b22ade0bbaa0259e06e9bb0.png)\n\nYou've probably realized by now that working with 3-tensors directly is cumbersome! So, rather than trying to visualize the full tensor all at once, focusing on its components is often helpful. Generally,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{A}} \\right)_{i, j, k} = \\dfrac{\\partial f_i}{\\partial a_{jk}}\n$$\n\nis a *scalar* representing the derivative of the $i$th output component with respect to the element $a_{jk}$ of the matrix $\\mathbf A$.\n\nFor example, the derivative of $f_{\\color{red}1}$ with respect to $a_{{\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}2}}$ corresponds to the component at index $({\\color{red}1},{\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}2})$ of a tensor. In our example, we saw that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nLooking at the ${\\color{red}1}$st layer (front face), row ${\\color{blue}3}$, column ${\\color{blue}2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf A}\\right)_{{\\color{red}1}, {\\color{blue}3}, {\\color{blue}2}} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}\n$$\n\nDerivatives of matrix functions arise in optimization problems, machine learning (e.g., backpropagation through layers), and physics (e.g., tensor fields). Understanding their structure is key to efficient computation and interpretation." 13194139535822 17592186045417 false] [17592186205216 1064 "Suppose we have the function $\\mathbf f: \\mathbb R^{3\\times2} \\to \\mathbb R^{2},$ defined as\n\n![](images/8d/8d7f629f4b82087a1d23ef1253ce7eb98ca760310d92a2ac8cf8c3fd3a4d9473.svg)\n\nWe calculate the derivatives of $\\mathbf f$ layer by layer as follows:\n\n- **Layer 1**: The derivatives of the first component $f_1$ with respect to $\\mathbf A.$\n![](images/c4/c4dab9446396e82d20ebbb38e3aabce9908565aeb7aeb48f2804a271d6ddf66f.svg)\n\n- **Layer 2**: The derivatives of the second component $f_2$ with respect to $\\mathbf A.$\n![](images/ce/ce7e31c8c3faf75aa7980a0e4f5f707f84f116e97b30c8b0dbd504bcfb4822c8.svg)\n\nCombining these layers into a 3D block, we get the total derivative (a $3$-tensor in $\\mathbb R^{2\\times3\\times2}$):\n\n![](images/3d/3d6eeebb92218d4f0995dbccd74b444da112f0e55b22ade0bbaa0259e06e9bb0.png)\n\nYou've probably realized by now that working with 3-tensors directly is cumbersome! So, rather than trying to visualize the full tensor all at once, focusing on its components is often helpful. Generally,\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{A}} \\right)_{i, j, k} = \\dfrac{\\partial f_i}{\\partial a_{jk}}\n$$\n\nis a *scalar* representing the derivative of the $i$th output component with respect to the element $a_{jk}$ of the matrix $\\mathbf A.$\n\nFor example, the derivative of $f_{\\color{red}1}$ with respect to $a_{{\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}2}}$ corresponds to the component at index $({\\color{red}1},{\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}2})$ of a tensor. In our example, we saw that\n![](images/4f/4fa3d21f5d62bd01491c49b3351cdd3cf751a7ad793509d1c2b3846e4f6825cd.svg)\n\nLooking at the ${\\color{red}1}$st layer (front face), row ${\\color{blue}3},$ colu